1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,950
ReqLens puts every requirement next to the part of your draft that answers it.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,950
Paste the rules and your draft, then check.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,950
Gemini lists the requirements. Code keeps a quote only if it is in the rules word for word.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,950
Each row shows the rule's sentence, the verdict and the draft sentence used as proof.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:49,950
What text cannot prove is set aside for you to confirm. No verdict is invented.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,950
The draft never said how its AI works. Add that paragraph and re-check.

7
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,950
Only the comparison runs again.

8
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:17,640
Verdicts that changed since the last check are marked.

9
00:01:17,690 --> 00:01:39,000
A harder case: this hackathon's own rules against our own early write-up. Met rows fold away so the gaps come first.

10
00:01:39,050 --> 00:01:54,330
After a rewrite, 15 verdicts changed. One requirement was still missing.

11
00:01:54,380 --> 00:02:10,770
When the model is overloaded, the requirement list is kept and the next try starts from the comparison.
